+original problem-only feature set. Establishing interpretability or private-task performance requires
+evaluation beyond this unscored sample panel. [3, 4]
+
+B     Reproduction and assistance
+The release supplies a hash-pinned CPU pipeline rather than additional model runs, with frozen
+reference baselines (a constant rule and a surface-feature control). The public reproduction repos-
+itory is https://anonymous.4open.science/r/aimo-identifiability. The v8.1 manuscript source base is
+[withheld for anonymous review]; the build provenance records subsequent text-only corrections.
+
+
+                                                   9
++It verifies the leaderboard snapshot and parquet inputs before computing; registers measured val-
+ues in a claims ledger; renders every reported measurement through checked numeric keys; and
+builds both author variants from one Markdown manuscript using the unchanged official NeurIPS
+style. The leaderboard tool records denominator fits, parsing resolutions, owner counts, corrected
+tails, exact power at the current grids and the sustained-threshold certification. The reproduction
+gates also test missing, early, mismatched and bad-hash receipt fixtures without treating them as real
+submissions.
+The archive includes immutable dataset revisions, file hashes and a downloader. It does not re-
+distribute third-party labelled data with unresolved licence terms. A separate publication record
+contains clean-clone checks, source/PDF hashes, page counts and render review. Those records es-
+tablish what was executed and which artifacts can be rebuilt; compilation alone does not validate the
+assumptions of the statistical model. The raw leaderboard retains source identifiers for auditability,
+while the scientific report discusses aggregated owner counts and neutral source titles.
+The practical result is a reproducible evidential boundary: under a declared calibration model, the
+visible validation grid separates only perfect observed outcomes after selection and multiplicity, and
+has low single-method power for the stated moderate effects. A larger confirmation set, item-level
+paired outcomes and explicit grouping/balance rules would make the board more informative about
+robustness predictors and about the interpretability claims attached to them.
